Mock syscall name tables
========================

The checked-in mock-syscalls.inc files are generated from Linux v6.6,
commit ffc253263a1375a65fa6c9f62a893e9767fbebfa. The x86 syscall tables
are the source used to generate the UAPI syscall numbers. The arm64 UAPI
header includes the generic UAPI syscall definitions. The generator checks
the SHA-256 of each of its four source files before producing output.

To regenerate without cloning a repository, download the four pinned files
to a source tree outside the user's home directory. For example:

    linux=/tmp/bst-linux-v6.6
    base=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/ffc253263a1375a65fa6c9f62a893e9767fbebfa
    for file in arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_32.tbl \
                arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_64.tbl \
                arch/arm64/include/uapi/asm/unistd.h \
                include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h; do
        mkdir -p "$linux/$(dirname "$file")"
        curl -fL "$base/$file" -o "$linux/$file"
    done

Then run from the bst root:

    python3 arch/gen-mock-syscalls.py "$linux"

Python 3 and a C preprocessor (cc, or --cc to select another) are needed.
No source download or host compatibility headers are needed to build bst.
The arm64 preprocessing environment fixes __BITS_PER_LONG at 64. The x32
table combines common and x32 entries, including the x32-specific numbers.
The generated numbers do not include x32's ABI bit; the parser adds it.
This metadata does not change the existing notification filters.
